NNAAMMEE
     ssttrrffiillee, uunnssttrr — create a random access file for storing strings

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssttrrffiillee [--CCiioorrssxx] [--cc _c_h_a_r] _s_o_u_r_c_e___f_i_l_e [_o_u_t_p_u_t___f_i_l_e]
     uunnssttrr _s_o_u_r_c_e___f_i_l_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssttrrffiillee utility reads a file containing groups of lines separated by
     a line containing a single percent ‘%’ sign and creates a data file which
     contains a header structure and a table of file offsets for each group of
     lines.  This allows random access of the strings.

     The output file, if not specified on the command line, is named
     _s_o_u_r_c_e___f_i_l_e_._d_a_t.

     The options are as follows:

     --CC       Flag the file as containing comments.  This option cases the
              STR_COMMENTS bit in the header _s_t_r___f_l_a_g_s field to be set.  Com‐
              ments are designated by two delimiter characters at the begin‐
              ning of the line, though ssttrrffiillee does not give any special
              treatment to comment lines.

     --cc _c_h_a_r  Change the delimiting character from the percent sign to _c_h_a_r.

     --ii       Ignore case when ordering the strings.

     --oo       Order the strings in alphabetical order.  The offset table will
              be sorted in the alphabetical order of the groups of lines ref‐
              erenced.  Any initial non-alphanumeric characters are ignored.
              This option causes the STR_ORDERED bit in the header _s_t_r___f_l_a_g_s
              field to be set.

     --rr       Randomize access to the strings.  Entries in the offset table
              will be randomly ordered.  This option causes the STR_RANDOM bit
              in the header _s_t_r___f_l_a_g_s field to be set.

     --ss       Run silently; do not give a summary message when finished.

     --xx       Note that each alphabetic character in the groups of lines is
              rotated 13 positions in a simple caesar cypher.  This option
              causes the STR_ROTATED bit in the header _s_t_r___f_l_a_g_s field to be
              set.

     The format of the header is:

     #define VERSION 1
     uint32_t        str_version;    /* version number */
     uint32_t        str_numstr;     /* # of strings in the file */
     uint32_t        str_longlen;    /* length of longest string */
     uint32_t        str_shortlen;   /* length of shortest string */
     #define STR_RANDOM      0x1     /* randomized pointers */
     #define STR_ORDERED     0x2     /* ordered pointers */
     #define STR_ROTATED     0x4     /* rot-13'd text */
     #define STR_COMMENTS    0x8     /* embedded comments */
     uint32_t        str_flags;      /* bit field for flags */
     char            str_delim;      /* delimiting character */

     All fields are written in network byte order.

     The purpose of uunnssttrr is to undo the work of ssttrrffiillee.  It prints out the
     strings contained in the file _s_o_u_r_c_e___f_i_l_e in the order that they are
     listed in the header file _s_o_u_r_c_e___f_i_l_e_._d_a_t to standard output.  It is pos‐
     sible to create sorted versions of input files by using --oo when ssttrrffiillee
     is run and then using uunnssttrr to dump them out in the table order.

FFIILLEESS
     strfile.dat  default output file.

